WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:06.380
In this unit we're going to talk about
the challenge of translating individual

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:12.350
statements or individual instructions
from symbolic to binary code.

3
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.100
Now the general problem that we
are facing is developing an assembler

4
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.630
that translates program from
symbolic hack language,

5
00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:27.270
into binary hack code and
in order to write such an assembler

6
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:33.140
we have to know how to deal with white
space, instructions and symbols.

7
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:38.560
Now in order to make our life easier we
decided to defer the treatment of symbols

8
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:45.620
to a later stage and we also described
previously how to handle white space.

9
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.460
Basically we're going
to ignore white space.

10
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.507
So, what remains, is a program
that contains instructions only,

11
00:00:52.507 --> 00:00:57.240
A-instructions and
C-instructions without symbols.

12
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.760
So, in order to write an assembler that
can translate such a programming to binary

13
00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.310
code we have to know how to translate
A-instructions and C-instructions.

14
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.100
And that's exactly what
we're going to discuss next.

15
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.250
So, let us begin with A-instructions.

16
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.620
Here's the general rule and

17
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:20.690
the syntax rule of how to put together
an A-instruction and an example.

18
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:26.430
And here's the same,
instruction expressed in the binary code.

19
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.790
Notice that,
the opcode of an A-instruction is zero.

20
00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:38.650
This is the first, red zero bit that you
see, in the example at the bottom right.

21
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.530
By the way,
the colors are completely meaningless,

22
00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:46.570
and I used them only to
improve our communications.

23
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.400
You know,
once it gets into actually implementation,

24
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.750
the colors once again
play no role whatsoever.

25
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:59.980
So how to translate from symbolic to
binary when it comes to an A-instruction.

26
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.940
Well if you think about it,

27
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.640
the only challenge is to do
something with this value.

28
00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.950
So basically if the value
is a decimal constant,

29
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:16.230
all we have to do is compute the binary
representation of this value.

30
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.859
Add as many zeroes as we need in order
to turn it into a 15 bit constant.

31
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.060
Ed or append, the zero opcode and,
that's what we, and, and that's what is a,

32
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.380
a needed 16 bit, representation of this,
of this instruction.

33
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.570
So it's not a big deal, but
obviously we have to do it.

34
00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.730
What if, value's a symbol?

35
00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.130
Well, I just want to, remind you,
what what we said earlier.

36
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.360
We're going to,
deal with symbols later on.

37
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:53.820
So, translating instructions is, is

38
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:59.130
something that's not terribly complicated,
but once again it takes, some programming.

39
00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.277
All right, what about C-instructions.

40
00:03:01.277 --> 00:03:05.950
Well, C-instructions also have a symbolic

41
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.930
manifestation and and
a binary manifestation.

42
00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:15.810
And there's a set of tables, so to speak,
that describe the mapping from.

43
00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:20.790
Symbolic pneumonics into
their binary representations.

44
00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:25.980
So these are the rules of the game when
it comes to translating C-instructions.

45
00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.750
So how do you actually do it?

46
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:32.520
I think the easier way to to describe
the translation process is to

47
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.840
do it using an example.

48
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.414
So suppose we're giving this single

49
00:03:39.414 --> 00:03:45.100
C-instruction and we have to
translate that into binary code.

50
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.060
Well, before we go on I want to
remind you that every C-instruction

51
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.690
consists of three fields.

52
00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.018
So, in the example that we have here.

53
00:03:55.018 --> 00:04:00.556
The MD pneumonic is
the destination field or

54
00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:07.560
the value of the destination field
in this particular instruction.

55
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.350
Then comes D plus 1,
which is the value of the comp or

56
00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:15.320
computational, computation field.

57
00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.330
And we also have a jump field
which happens to be null.

58
00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:24.380
You know, we don't have a jump
instruction here so the jump is a null.

59
00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:29.420
So implicit in what I'm saying
is the assumption that we can

60
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:35.990
somehow take this source instruction and
decompose it into these three fields.

61
00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.890
And indeed this is something that is going
to be done by an element of our assembler

62
00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.280
called parser.

63
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.790
The parser is going to
take a source statement or

64
00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:53.100
instruction written in symbolic code and
chop it into three individual,

65
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:57.890
individual fields, and then we can inspect
every one of these fields in isolation.

66
00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:01.510
And act on it, and
that's exactly what I'm going to do next.

67
00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:08.620
So, let us begin to,
put together the string of characters

68
00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:14.540
that will end up representing the binary
code of MD equals D plus one.

69
00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.389
We can do it in, in many different ways,
and here is one of them.

70
00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.810
Focusing on the target expression

71
00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:28.500
we see that every C-instruction
begins with three ones.

72
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.610
When it comes to to the binary
flavor of C-instructions, so

73
00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.440
we initialize the string
that we are building.

74
00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.600
With three ones.

75
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.470
That's, that's what we
do when we get started.

76
00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:47.530
Next we focus, if we want, we focus on the
next seven bits that we have to create.

77
00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.500
And these seven bits
correspond to the comp field.

78
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.840
Now, the comp field
happens to be D plus one.

79
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.120
Once again, I assume that I have access
to this, field, I can easily retrieve it.

80
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.450
I see that it's D plus one.

81
00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:06.440
And if it's D plus one I can look
it up in the relevant table.

82
00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:10.810
I look up this pneumonic, and
I see that it corresponds to, an a bit,

83
00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:16.650
which is zero, and to six control
bits which are zero and five ones.

84
00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:20.780
So putting these bits together I generate

85
00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:25.170
the seven bit value zero
zero one one one one one and

86
00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:31.090
I append it to the string
that I gradually build here.

87
00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.180
Alrl ight.

88
00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.940
The next thing to do is focus on

89
00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.650
the next part of the instruction
which corresponds to the destination.

90
00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.350
So, I look up the value
of my destination field.

91
00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:45.520
It happens to be MD.

92
00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:50.230
I consult the relevant, table.

93
00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:54.360
And I see that the MD
pneumonic corresponds to 011.

94
00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:00.600
I take this 011 value, I append it to
this string that I gradually built,

95
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:09.410
and I've completed the the synthesis
of these two fields together.

96
00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:14.780
Moving along the remaining three bits
correspond to the jump directive.

97
00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:18.660
I look up the value of the jump
directive in my source instruction.

98
00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.700
I see that it's null.

99
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.290
I look up null in the relevant table and

100
00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:27.450
I see that it corresponds to
the three bits zero, zero, zero.

101
00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:32.350
I take these bits, append them to
the end of the string and voila,

102
00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:39.180
we have managed to translate the symbolic
instruction into its binary equivalent.

103
00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:46.020
As you see, everything that they
do here is text processing,

104
00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.930
and in, in some languages it
is called string processing,

105
00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:54.620
I have a source string which
I analyze in some way and,

106
00:07:54.620 --> 00:08:01.000
I have a target string which I build,
you know, in some gradual process.

107
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.750
And, every high level language
has the capability to

108
00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.960
to do such as string processing.

109
00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.260
And, that's exactly what you'll do
when you actually write the assembler.

110
00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:16.640
Now, when we say that we
generate binary code.

111
00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.640
There may be some misunderstanding here so
let me clarify it.

112
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:25.990
We basically generate a text
file that contains of

113
00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.000
that which is, which consists of two
characters only zero and one but

114
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.170
they are treated as characters as SD or
Unicode characters.

115
00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:40.580
So we have this text file that we write
which consists of zeros and ones only.

116
00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.680
And later on we're going to load
this text file into the computer.

117
00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:50.820
And once we load it into the computer it
becomes real zeros and ones so to speak.

118
00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.370
All right so this basically concludes.

119
00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.900
The translation of C-instructions.

120
00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:01.150
So with that in mind,
here is the overall assembly logic.

121
00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:06.620
We are given a text file, and this text
file contains all sorts of characters

122
00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:12.220
that hopefully represent a Hack
program written in symbolic Hack code.

123
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.730
So, how do we translate it into binary?

124
00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:19.050
Well, we process this file.

125
00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:23.330
And, we begin marching through it.

126
00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.140
And for each instruction, or for

127
00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:30.350
each line in this file,
we first of all parse the instruction.

128
00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:33.640
We break it into its underlying fields.

129
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:39.450
So if we have an A-instruction
we have the at sign and a value.

130
00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:44.670
We take this value and
we re-express it in binary code.

131
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:50.085
If it's a C-instruction we split
the instruction into its three

132
00:09:51.225 --> 00:09:56.165
fields comp, desk, and
and jump, and also for

133
00:09:56.165 --> 00:10:01.580
every one of these fields we generate the,
corresponding

134
00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:07.660
binary binary bits, and
then we assemble all these codes that we,

135
00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:11.840
created into a single 16 bit instruction.

136
00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.980
And we write this instruction
to an output file.

137
00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.150
Which is not seen here in the picture.

138
00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.970
But, the assembler creates this
empty file at the beginning.

139
00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:26.490
And begins to populate it with, lines.

140
00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:33.010
Each line being a sequence of, or
a string of 16 zeros and ones.

141
00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:37.720
So this is the overall process
of translating a Hack program.

142
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.060
A program that consists of only A and
C-instructions without symbols.

143
00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:49.190
If you do all this,
then you will manage to create

144
00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:53.620
an assembler that can translate
programs into binary code and

145
00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:59.820
everything looks very nice indeed,
and I'd hate to be,

146
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:05.440
a party spoiler, but there's a big
gap that we haven't closed yet,

147
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.350
and this is the fact that the source
code contains no symbols.

148
00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:18.010
We have managed to to design an assembler
that translates a symbol-less programs,

149
00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:22.610
but we still have to close this
gap of dealing with symbols, and

150
00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:26.130
that's exactly what we're
going to do in the next unit.